 "provenance": {
  "physics_version": 7,
  "physics_tag": "lin2010-hybrid-aberrated-shocked-imf-drawnclip2d-seedsafe",
  "magnetopause": "Lin et al. 2010 (doi:10.1029/2009JA014235) 3-D surface - cusp indentations, dipole-tilt and dawn-dusk asymmetry - on a subsolar distance blended C2 to Shue et al. 1998 outside Lin's fitted box (Bz -15..+5 nT, Pd 1..16 nPa), where Lin's inputs are clamped",
  "bow_shock": "Slavin & Holzer 1981 conic, Farris & Russell 1994 standoff, ratio bounded at BS_RATIO_MAX, radius scaled per azimuth by the magnetopause's own shape so the sheath cannot invert",
  "aberration": "applied to the magnetopause evaluation and the wind's upstream direction only - Tsyganenko field lines stay in plain GSM, the frame they are fitted in",
  "aurora": "Modeled auroral oval - Kp 9 (statistical Kp model, not an observation)",
  "key_moments": "KEY MOMENTS: shock 14:45 UT 15 Jul (6 -> 24 nPa) | Bz min -60 nT at 19:55 UT 15 Jul | wind peaks 1137 km/s 22:05 UT 15 Jul | Kp 9 from 18:35 UT 15 Jul | SYM-H min -326 nT at 21:55 UT 15 Jul | Bz below -10 nT for 6 h",
  "talking_points": [
   "WATCH: the CME shock from the X5.7 flare hits L1 about 14:37 UT on 15 Jul - the pressure jumps roughly tenfold and the magnetopause and bow shock crush inward within a step",
   "PHYSICS: Bz swings to about -60 nT. Southward IMF reconnects at the nose, dayside field lines peel back into the tail - watch the tail lines stretch and the oval expand",
   "IMPACT: Kp 9, Dst about -300 nT. Aurora seen as far south as Texas - the ASCA X-ray satellite lost attitude control and never observed again, radio and GPS were disrupted"
  ],
  "decimation": "Every layer is SUBSAMPLED for the web, never resampled: vertices are a strict subset of the rendered ones. Densities are therefore lower than the physics produced, and should be described as a subsample rather than as the modelled density.",
  "occluded_points": "Dot layers drop points that never leave 1 R_E: they sit inside Earth and are occluded in any renderer that draws it. Measured on Gannon: the Plasmasphere layer fills a solid ball from the geocentre outward, so 57% of its dots were in that category at the storm peak. Per-layer counts are in layers[].occluded_dropped.",
  "field_pulses": "Not shipped. Beads gliding along +B are reconstructed in the shader from the FieldLines vertices they ride on.",
  "surfaces": "MagnetopauseShell, BowShockShell, RingCurrent and Plasmasphere are triangulated surfaces (kind 'mesh', a faces block per layer, color0 = the dome's holder colour). The ring current is a torus whose strength is the measured SYM-H energy but whose radius is climatology; the plasmasphere radius IS the measurement (Carpenter & Anderson 1992).",
  "credits": "Engine/docs/CREDITS.html carries the full reference list with DOIs."
 },
